4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3],[3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3],[3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3],[3,3,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,3,3,3,3,3,4,1,1,1,1,1,1,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,1,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,1,3,3,3,3,3,4,1,1,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The input has three parts:\n\n- **A large shape.** In pair 1 it is the 3-coloured shape in rows 1–4.\n- **A marker line.** This is a line of single dots right next to the shape, in a different colour. In pair 1 it is row 5, with 2-coloured dots.\n- **A second dotted line.** It sits further away and has the shape's colour. In pair 1 it is row 11, with 3-coloured dots.\n\n**How the output is built.** The output is the input plus a mirror copy of the shape on the far side of the second line, drawn in the marker colour.\n\n- **Pair 1.** The new 2-coloured shape fills rows 12–15. Shape row 4 maps to row 12 and row 1 maps to row 15, so row r goes to row 16 − r. That is a reflection about the midline between the two dot lines: (5 + 11)/2 = 8, so r → 2·8 − r. The marker line (row 5) lands exactly on the second line (row 11).\n- **Pair 2.** The same thing happens on its side. The 1-coloured shape fills columns 1–3, the 2-coloured markers are in column 4, and the 1-coloured dots are in column 10. Column c maps to 14 − c, so column 3 → 11 and column 1 → 13. This matches the fact sheet's added cells, which lie in columns 11–13. All of them are colour 2, the marker colour.\n\nSo the rule is:\n1. Take the largest object as the shape.\n2. Every other non-background cell is a dot. If the dots fall in exactly 2 columns, the lines are vertical; otherwise they are horizontal rows.\n3. The line nearer the shape is the marker line, and its colour is the paint colour.\n4. Reflect each shape cell across the axis halfway between the two lines (new coordinate = a + b − old) and paint it with the marker colour. Only background cells get painted.\n\n**Test.** The 4-coloured shape fills columns 2–7. The 1-coloured markers are in column 8 and the 4-coloured dots are in column 14. Column c maps to 22 − c, so the mirrored shape occupies columns 15–20 in colour 1. That fits inside the 22-wide grid.\n\nThe edit is a single stage: stamp the mirrored, recoloured shape.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    shape = max(objs, key=lambda o: o[\"size\"])\n    sset = set(shape[\"cells\"])\n    dots = [(y, x) for y in range(g.shape[0]) for x in range(g.shape[1])\n            if g[y, x] != bg and (y, x) not in sset]\n    cols = sorted(set(x for _, x in dots))\n    rows = sorted(set(y for y, _ in dots))\n    vertical = len(cols) == 2\n    lines = cols if vertical else rows\n    a, b = lines\n    # marker line = the one closest to the shape\n    sc = [c[1] if vertical else c[0] for c in shape[\"cells\"]]\n    da = min(abs(v - a) for v in sc)\n    db = min(abs(v - b) for v in sc)\n    marker = a if da <= db else b\n    mc = [g[y, x] for y, x in dots if (x if vertical else y) == marker][0]\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    for y, x in shape[\"cells\"]:\n        if vertical:\n            ny, nx = y, a + b - x\n        else:\n            ny, nx = a + b - y, x\n        if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W and out[ny, nx] == bg:\n            out[ny, nx] = mc\n    emit(out, \"Mirror the big shape across the midline between the two dotted lines, \"\n              \"painting the copy in the colour of the marker line next to the shape.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Take the largest object as the shape."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The edit is a single stage: stamp the mirrored, recoloured shape."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How the output is built.** The output is the input plus a mirror copy of the shape on the far side of the second line, drawn in the marker colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"That is a reflection about the midline between the two dot lines: (5 + 11)/2 = 8, so r → 2·8 − r."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Reflect each shape cell across the axis halfway between the two lines (new coordinate = a + b − old) and paint it with the marker colour."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The edit is a single stage: stamp the mirrored, recoloured shape."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"If the dots fall in exactly 2 columns, the lines are vertical; otherwise they are horizontal rows."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"db354877455920b24868a1bacc2536277ab8e84df33cf2091a07aab53fe42f32","task_sha256":"50c8673cf876e97571f3d30fac06e48dd266ec8e00c9b4ac66c63f289318c10c","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
